kely R or safer; prediction markets price Schmidt near 88 percent", "No public general-election polling, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, ratings, and money", "Don Coover's near-parity fundraising (about $1.2M raised) and national Democratic target-list attention keep the race off the safe column"], "rationale": "A district roughly ten points right of the nation, an incumbent Republican, and unanimous expert ratings at Likely R or safer make Schmidt a clear favorite, with markets in agreement. Coover's strong fundraising and Democratic midterm bullishness justify Likely R rather than Safe R, but money alone rarely closes a double-digit structural gap without polling evidence.", "watch": "Any late independent polling putting Coover within single digits, or significant outside spending entering the district against Schmidt, would signal a genuine race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Derek Schmidt (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kansas' 2nd District is a Republican-leaning seat, roughly ten points right of the country, and incumbent Derek Schmidt is seeking re-election after securing the GOP nomination in August. All ten major race ratings place the contest at Likely R or safer, and prediction markets price Schmidt's chances around 88 percent. No public general-election polling is available, so the picture rests on district fundamentals, ratings, and campaign finance.\n\nDemocrat Don Coover has mounted a well-funded challenge, raising about 1.2 million dollars and keeping pace with Schmidt, and headlines show national Democrats adding the seat to their midterm target lists. That financial strength is the main reason some raters stop at Likely R rather than Safe R. Still, a challenger with no polling support in a district this red remains a clear underdog, and a Libertarian candidate is also on the ballot.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0506, "m